# Container image for the Pleiades runner: the stateless worker that
# pulls jobs off a durable NATS consumer group and executes them
# (cmd/runner).
#
# Two stages. The builder compiles with cgo against glibc; the runtime is
# a distroless glibc image with no shell and no package manager. This is
# deliberately Dockerfile.controller with a different binary and no
# published port, so a change to one belongs in the other too. The one
# place the two now differ on purpose is the writable directory: the
# controller has one and this image has none, and the runtime stage below
# says why.

# Debian bookworm, not alpine, and the choice is forced rather than
# stylistic. cmd/runner links github.com/mattn/go-sqlite3, which is a cgo
# driver, so the binary is dynamically linked against the C library it was
# built with. Alpine is musl and the runtime image below is glibc, so a
# musl-linked binary cannot start there at all. Turning cgo off to dodge
# that is the trap explained at the build step.
#
# Pinned by digest as well as by tag. A tag is a moving pointer that can
# name a different image tomorrow; only the digest names one exact image,
# so two people building on different days get the same compiler. This is
# the stronger form of the rule docker-compose.yml already follows by
# pinning exact versions.
FROM golang:1.26-bookworm@sha256:116d58cbd88c1297624acc6e967a060012422bacf9930927e23fb719189c6f36 AS builder

WORKDIR /src

# The module definition on its own, ahead of the source, so that the
# dependency download lands in a layer that editing a .go file does not
# invalidate.
COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download

COPY . .

# The final image's directory tree is assembled here, in the one stage
# that still has a shell. The runtime image below has none: no RUN, no
# mkdir, no chown can ever run there, so anything the running container
# needs has to exist before the COPY that carries it across.
#
# The runner resolves RUNBOOK_DIR the same way the controller does and
# exits if the directory is missing, so the image needs the same path to
# exist. It is read-only in both images: internal/runbook.NewDirSource
# stats, lists and reads, and creates nothing.
#
# What an image cannot do is share it. The old version of this comment
# said "production shares one runbook directory between them", as though
# creating the same path in two images made it one directory. It does not:
# each image carries its own empty /app/runbooks, and two containers built
# from them get two separate copies. Sharing takes a mount, the same
# source at this same path in both services, and docker-compose.yml now
# makes that mount (read-only, since neither binary writes here). Without
# it this container could execute only the runbooks baked into its image,
# which is none of them, while the controller happily launched jobs
# against runbooks it could see and this side could not.
RUN mkdir -p /staging/app/runbooks

# Built as a PACKAGE path, not a single file. A single-file build
# silently stops compiling the moment the composition root is split
# across more than one file, while `make ci` (go build ./...) stays
# green, so the image breaks and nothing else does.
#
# CGO_ENABLED=1 is set explicitly because the wrong value here fails in
# the worst possible way. With cgo off the build still succeeds, and
# go-sqlite3 links a stub that only fails when a query runs, so the
# failure surfaces at some later moment rather than at build time. The
# controller image proves the point loudly, dying inside its first schema
# migration, and the two images are built the same way on purpose. That is
# exactly what the previous alpine builder shipped: the official
# golang:*-alpine images set CGO_ENABLED=0 for you, because they carry no
# C compiler.
#
# -trimpath strips build-machine filesystem paths out of the binary, so
# the same source produces the same output regardless of where it was
# built. -s -w drop the symbol table and the DWARF debug info, which are
# a large share of the binary's size and are of no use in a production
# container. Neither flag changes behavior.
#
# What this build cannot do is stamp the commit. `go build` reads
# vcs.revision out of .git, and .dockerignore keeps .git out of the build
# context on purpose: it is a 60 MB non-input, and the deny-by-default
# shape that excludes it is the same control that keeps .SPECIFICATION
# out. So the commit travels in as a build argument and lands in an OCI
# label instead. See the label block in the runtime stage.
#
# One consequence worth knowing: cgo means this stage cannot cross
# compile. It builds for the architecture it runs on.
RUN CGO_ENABLED=1 go build -trimpath -ldflags="-s -w" -o /staging/app/runner ./cmd/runner

# gcr.io/distroless/base-debian12 holds glibc, CA certificates and
# tzdata, and essentially nothing else. There is no shell, no package
# manager and no busybox, so a process that escapes into this container
# finds no interactive foothold and nothing to pivot through.
#
# distroless/static and scratch were both rejected for one reason: they
# ship no libc, and the cgo SQLite driver linked above needs one. The
# :nonroot tag is the same image with its user already set to UID 65532,
# so the image cannot be run as root by accident.
#
# Digest-pinned for the same reason the builder is.
FROM gcr.io/distroless/base-debian12:nonroot@sha256:b12529fbbd0bb15eea8905f69d83148679e0b4d7d434c8808100792029b1caae

# Provenance, which this image carried none of before: no labels and, for
# the reason given at the build step, no Go VCS stamp either, so nothing
# in a built image said which commit produced it.
#
# Defaults are present so a bare `docker build` still succeeds and says
# plainly that it does not know, rather than failing or, worse, claiming a
# revision it never had. A build that wants real provenance passes them:
#
#   docker build -f Dockerfile.runner \
#     --build-arg VCS_REF="$(git rev-parse HEAD)" \
#     --build-arg VERSION="$(git describe --tags --always --dirty)" \
#     -t pleiades-runner:dev .
#
# Read them back with:
#
#   docker inspect -f '{{json .Config.Labels}}' pleiades-runner:dev
ARG VCS_REF=unknown
ARG VERSION=unknown
ARG SOURCE=https://github.com/Subject-Void-LLC/the-pleiades

# GPL-3.0-only is the SPDX identifier for the LICENSE file at the root of
# this repository. Nothing in this project grants the "or any later
# version" option, so the label must not claim it; if that ever changes,
# this string changes with it.
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="Pleiades Runner" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="Pleiades execution plane: stateless worker pulling dispatches off a durable NATS consumer group." \
      org.opencontainers.image.licenses="GPL-3.0-only" \
      org.opencontainers.image.source="${SOURCE}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.revision="${VCS_REF}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.version="${VERSION}"

# Root-owned and world readable and executable, which is the correction to
# what this image used to do. It used to copy the tree with
# --chown=nonroot:nonroot, so /app and /app/runner were mode 0755 owned by
# 65532, the same uid the process runs as. A compromised process could not
# overwrite the running binary (the kernel refuses a write to a running
# executable's text), but it did not need to: it could unlink the file and
# write its own in place, and the next start would run that. That survives
# `docker compose restart`, `docker restart`, a restart policy and a host
# reboot, because it is the same container's writable layer, so one
# process compromise became a permanent one. Root-owned 0755 removes the
# class outright: 65532 may read and execute, and may not unlink.
#
# This matters here as much as in the controller and arguably more: the
# native adapter re-executes THIS binary once per task to run a collection
# method in its own subprocess (internal/adapters/native's IPC boundary),
# so the file is read back off disk repeatedly during normal operation,
# and a replaced one would be picked up without any restart at all.
COPY --from=builder /staging/app /app

# No writable directory at all, and that is the one deliberate difference
# from Dockerfile.controller.
#
# The controller has /data because two of its defaults are relative paths
# (a SQLite database and a credential directory) that have to land
# somewhere writable. This process has neither. It opens no database, and
# the only thing on its execution path that writes to its own filesystem
# at all is the result WAL, which is opt-in through RUNNER_WAL_DIR and
# stays off unless an operator sets it and mounts something for it to
# write to. An empty writable directory that nothing uses is just a place
# for a compromised process to stage files, so there is not one.
#
# A deployment that turns the WAL on has to give RUNNER_WAL_DIR an
# ABSOLUTE path onto a mount owned by 65532. A relative one resolves
# against the working directory below, which this uid cannot write to, and
# runner.NewFileWAL fails closed at startup rather than losing results
# quietly later.
WORKDIR /app
# Numeric, never the name "nonroot". Kubernetes refuses to start a
# container whose USER is a name when the pod sets
# securityContext.runAsNonRoot: true, because the kubelet has no
# /etc/passwd to resolve the name against: CreateContainerConfigError,
# "image has non-numeric user (nonroot), cannot verify user is non-root".
# Compose cannot see this, which is why it shipped. Same reasoning and same
# identity as Dockerfile.controller; 65532 is what the distroless nonroot
# tag already uses.
USER 65532:65532

# No EXPOSE. The runner binds no port at all: it dials out to NATS and
# pulls work from a durable consumer group, so nothing ever connects to
# it. An EXPOSE here would document a listener that does not exist.

# A trap worth recording before someone falls into it, because it will
# look like a runtime bug rather than a permissions one.
#
# This container runs as UID 65532, and a host Docker socket is normally
# mode 0660 root:docker. The legacy Ansible path starts a sibling
# container over that socket (internal/adapters/legacy's
# DockerOrchestrator), so the day anybody mounts /var/run/docker.sock in
# to enable it, this process gets "permission denied" on connect rather
# than any message about a missing socket. Nothing mounts it today, so
# this is not a regression, it is the next person's afternoon. The fix is
# to give the container the socket's group
# (--group-add "$(stat -c '%g' /var/run/docker.sock)"), never to run this
# image as root.

# There is deliberately no HEALTHCHECK instruction, which matches
# Dockerfile.controller. That is now a choice about WHO OWNS THE PROBE
# rather than a statement that none exists, and the old version of this
# comment (which said this binary has no HTTP surface so there is nothing
# in-container to ask) has stopped being true.
#
# This binary can answer for itself as of Phase 20: `/app/runner
# healthcheck` reads the liveness heartbeat the agent writes while its
# durable NATS consumer is genuinely reachable, and exits non-zero when
# that file has gone stale (internal/runner/heartbeat.go says what a beat
# proves and what it misses). No HTTP surface was added to get there; the
# process still binds no port, which is why there is still no EXPOSE
# above.
#
# The probe stays out of the image because the orchestrator owns the
# timings. docker-compose.yml declares a healthcheck against this command
# and helm/the-pleiades wires it to the runner Deployment's liveness and
# readiness probes, each with intervals suited to its own scheduler. A
# HEALTHCHECK baked in here would be a third set of timings that neither
# of them can see, and it would apply on a `docker run` where no
# supervisor is watching the answer.
#
# THE WRITABLE PATH THIS NEEDS. The heartbeat defaults to
# /tmp/pleiades-runner/heartbeat, and this image still creates no writable
# directory (the runtime stage below says why). That is deliberate: /tmp
# is writable in every arrangement that matters (compose mounts a tmpfs,
# the chart mounts an emptyDir, a plain `docker run` without --read-only
# has an ordinary writable layer), and a deployment that has none can turn
# the heartbeat off by setting RUNNER_HEARTBEAT_FILE to an empty value.
# The binary fails closed at startup rather than discovering an unwritable
# path on its first beat.

CMD ["/app/runner"]
